All done — the round's work is complete: attribution delivered, the candidate fix built/measured/refuted with held-lease pairs, code reverted to byte-identical r7 (verified via `.text` compare), full gate battery green with the exact historical values, wisdom keys stripped under flock, and the strategy record has its Round gen_r8 section.

**Technique ended on: GT-PFA two-sweep with volume-major owned chain, unchanged from r7 after measurement (r8 = attribution round: node calibrated at 2×zmm-FMA + 1×shuffle with a ~2.1 uops/cycle global cap; the rolled-codelet frontend diet was built and refuted +11%/+3% at 50/100, so the saturated r7 code ships); best node numbers this session: B=1 L=100 4592 us/xform (board 4475), batched 161.3 us (L=40 B=8) and 421.2 us (L=50 B=4); rel L2 = 3.6–4.5e-16 single-call, chains 3.8/5.0/4.2e-14 vs tol 1e-10, bit-repeatable.**

Context for the record: the four-rounds-queued "port 5 vs DRAM" question about p1 was settled with a leased-core microbenchmark and rdtscp phase counters (PMU is locked; uiCA's install is incomplete and llvm-mca's ICX model turned out to dispatch 512-bit FMA to port 0 only, which the microbench disproved — 2.0 FMA/cycle on ports 0+5). The hot subpass loops run exactly at the node's ~2.1 vector-uops/cycle dispatch cap, so total uops — not ports, not DRAM, not instruction fetch — bind the compute passes; a 4× code-size cut moved nothing, and the added index uops of the rolled form cost 11% at L=50. With map placement, schedule, c-bypass, two-axes, frontend, and staging all now measured or closed by arithmetic, I recorded the engine as saturated on this host and shipped instruction-identical r7 code, protecting all three led cells (40: 160.0, 50: 414.0, 100: 4475.3 on the r7 board).
